<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Copyright 2026 boat1470
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0

  A roof over the mouth of the tunnel, flush with the walls that are already
  there.

  The walls exist. turtlebot3_autorace_2020.world defines tunnel_wall inline
  rather than through an <include>, which is why a search for a model
  directory or for `model://` found nothing and a first pass here concluded -
  wrongly, and said so in a comment - that there was no tunnel at all. It is
  four walls, each 0.05 m thick and 0.25 m tall, and its own pose offsets
  every link:

    model pose  (-0.964699, -1.03574)

    Wall_1   x -1.946   y -1.886 .. -0.086     west
    Wall_2   y -1.971   x -1.865 .. -0.065     south
    Wall_3   x -0.021   y -1.621 .. -0.084     east
    Wall_4   y -0.121   x -1.605 .. -0.025     north

  So it encloses roughly 1.93 x 1.85 m, and the way in is the gap in the north
  side between Wall_4's west end at x -1.605 and Wall_1 at x -1.946: 0.34 m
  across. The lane runs straight into it - measured off the course texture,
  white at x -1.873 and yellow at x -1.623, so the centre is x -1.748 - and
  the sign that announces it stands at (-1.544, 0.00) facing +/-y. Past that
  line the texture carries no paint at all, which is what a tunnel looks like
  from above.

  What is missing is the roof, and that is all this is. An earlier attempt
  here built its own pair of side walls as well; they ran straight through
  Wall_1 and Wall_4.

    x -1.971 .. -1.605   from Wall_1's outer face to Wall_4's west end
    y -0.096 .. -0.596   from Wall_4's inner face, 0.50 m in
    z  0.250 .. 0.260    resting on top of walls that are 0.25 m tall

  Nothing overhangs: both sides sit on wall, and the near edge is the wall
  it butts against.

  Height. The Burger is 0.192 m tall and its LDS sits at z 0.171, so the roof
  clears it by 58 mm and the beam passes under the plate and into the walls,
  which is where it is wanted - the camera loses the lane lines in here and
  the LiDAR gains something to measure against.

  On the light: a roof does not make it dark by itself. Measured with a plate
  and no walls, the frame under it was BRIGHTER than at the mouth - 115.7,
  99.8, 86.2, 96.2 going in - because light came in from every side and the
  camera, at z 0.10, was looking out sideways at open sky. Here the walls are
  what close it in; the plate only stops the sky.

  Spawned from full.launch.py rather than added to the world file, which
  belongs to the example. On by default, because the tunnel is open to the sky
  without it:

      ros2 launch arx_mission full.launch.py                # roof is built
      ros2 launch arx_mission full.launch.py roof:=false    # course as shipped

  Static, because nothing should be able to push it, and because a static
  model needs no inertia and no joints.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <model name="tunnel_roof">
    <static>true</static>
    <!-- Spawned on (-1.788, -0.346), the middle of the plate, so the numbers
         in full.launch.py are a position and not an offset to work out. -->
    <link name="plate">
      <pose>0 0 0.255 0 0 0</pose>
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.366 0.500 0.010</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.366 0.500 0.010</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.05 0.06 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.12 0.12 0.14 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.01 0.01 0.01 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
